package com.thanatos.recoveryapp;

public class LessonStore4 {
    public static String get(String topic) {
        if (topic == null) return null;
        switch (topic) {

            case "Interoceptive Prediction and Allostasis":
                return "INTEROCEPTIVE PREDICTION AND ALLOSTASIS\n\n"
                    + "WHAT IT IS\nTwo ideas that change how you think about the body and emotion. Allostasis is how the body stays stable by changing, anticipating needs before they arrive. Interoceptive prediction is the idea that your brain doesn't just receive signals from your body, it forecasts them.\n\n"
                    + "HOMEOSTASIS VS ALLOSTASIS\nHomeostasis is holding a variable steady around a set point, like body temperature. Allostasis, a term coined by Sterling and Eyer in 1988, means achieving stability through change: your blood pressure, hormones, and heart rate shift up and down all day to meet demands. The brain anticipates those demands (standing up, a hard conversation, a big meal) and adjusts ahead of time rather than waiting for an error.\n\n"
                    + "THE PREDICTION IDEA\nA leading framework in neuroscience, called predictive processing, proposes that the brain constantly generates predictions about incoming signals and updates them when they miss. Applied to the body, this is interoceptive inference: the insula and anterior cingulate cortex are thought to hold predictions about your internal state and compare them with actual signals from the heart, gut, lungs, and so on. Researchers such as Lisa Feldman Barrett, Kyle Simmons, and Anil Seth have argued that emotions and the feeling of 'how I feel' arise from this process. This is an influential theory, not settled fact. The evidence is real but indirect, and researchers still debate the details.\n\n"
                    + "WHAT IT MEANS IN PRACTICE\nIf feelings are partly predictions, then the same racing heart can be read as 'I'm afraid' or 'I'm excited,' depending on context and past learning. Studies on reappraising arousal found that people told to see pre-test anxiety as helpful performed better in some experiments, though results vary. The practical lesson: your reading of the signal matters, and that reading is trainable.\n\n"
                    + "ALLOSTATIC LOAD AND ADDICTION\nMcEwen's allostatic load is the wear that builds up when stress systems run hot for too long. Koob and Le Moal proposed that addiction involves allostatic change in reward and stress systems: the baseline shifts, so a person needs substances not to feel good but to feel normal. This model is widely cited and fits imaging and animal data, though like any model it simplifies. It explains why early recovery can feel flat, anxious, or irritable, and why that tends to ease as the system resets.\n\n"
                    + "CAN THOUGHT AND IMAGINATION CHANGE BODY STATE?\nYes, within limits. Vivid imagery measurably changes heart rate, sweating, and muscle tension. Placebo effects change pain and stress responses, and cognitive reappraisal changes both the experience and the physiology of a stressor. Heart rate variability biofeedback and slow breathing can shift autonomic balance. What thought can't do is override the core set points of survival: you can't think your way out of needing sleep, food, or oxygen. And claims of voluntary control over things like immunity or deep homeostatic regulation are mostly stronger than the evidence. The solid finding is that attention, interpretation, and imagery can nudge arousal and emotion, and that practice makes the nudges more reliable.\n\n"
                    + "INTEROCEPTIVE ACCURACY\nPeople differ in how well they sense their heartbeat, often tested by asking them to count beats without feeling their pulse. Links between this and anxiety, mood, and substance use exist but are mixed and depend on how it's measured. Mindfulness and body-based training can improve body awareness in several studies, though effects on accuracy tests are inconsistent.\n\n"
                    + "WHY IT MATTERS IN RECOVERY\nA craving often shows up first as a body state your brain has learned to read as 'I need to use.' Learn to feel it, label it more precisely, and test the interpretation, and the same signal can be reread as 'my body is asking for relief, and I have other ways to give it.' Regular sleep, movement, food, and calm routines also help your body's predictions settle, because a predictable day makes for a more predictable internal state.\n\n"
                    + "TRY THIS\n1. Body scan twice a day: spend a minute noticing heartbeat, breath, stomach, jaw, and hands without judging them.\n2. Label the state precisely: not 'bad' but 'tight chest, restless, and I'm anticipating a hard call.'\n3. Test the story: what else could this signal mean? Excitement, hunger, caffeine, tiredness, or stress?\n4. Use a body tool to change the signal: slow breathing with long exhales, a walk, or muscle relaxation.\n5. Keep your days predictable: regular sleep, meals, and movement give your system fewer surprises to react to.\n6. Rehearse in your imagination: picture handling a trigger calmly, step by step. Mental rehearsal builds the plan, and it doesn't replace real practice.\n7. Track it for two weeks and watch how your baseline changes.";

            default:
                return null;
        }
    }
}
